|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 5           | 4              |
| Low      | 1           | class-level    |
| **Total**| **10**      | **7 of 8**     |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH

##### 1. Conditional Test Logic — `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 31)

```csharp
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

One branch is never executed per run, hiding a gap. The test always passes but only verifies one of the two statuses.

**Fix — split into two deterministic tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_StandardOrder_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-CHEAP", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_PremiumOrder_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 5) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One status path is never verified — a regression on that path would go undetected.

---

##### 2. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)

```csharp
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
// no assertions
```

This test passes even if `ProcessOrder` returns garbage — only an unhandled exception would fail it.

**Fix — assert on the expected outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — code coverage increases but no behavior is verified.

---

##### 3. Sleepy Test — `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 98)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(2000);
```

Adds 2 seconds of wall-clock time per run. If the system is slower than expected the test becomes flaky; if faster, it wastes time.

**Fix — use a deterministic wait or a synchronization primitive:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent()
